WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.472
Allocation can fail, and safe C treats
that possibility as part of the design.

2
00:00:04.552 --> 00:00:07.241
A returned pointer must be checked before
it is used.

3
00:00:07.321 --> 00:00:10.927
If allocation fails, the function should
return a predictable error,

4
00:00:11.007 --> 00:00:13.329
preserve existing state where appropriate,

5
00:00:13.409 --> 00:00:16.037
and avoid using or releasing memory it
does not own.

6
00:00:16.117 --> 00:00:19.662
Allocated storage and initialized objects
are different concepts.

7
00:00:19.742 --> 00:00:22.736
Some allocation functions provide
uninitialized bytes,

8
00:00:22.816 --> 00:00:24.405
and some provide zeroed bytes.

9
00:00:24.485 --> 00:00:26.380
Zero-initialization can be useful,

10
00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:29.088
but it is not always the same as a valid
object state.

11
00:00:29.168 --> 00:00:33.018
The program still needs to establish
invariants such as length, capacity,

12
00:00:33.098 --> 00:00:35.420
ownership flags, and nested pointer
fields.

13
00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:38.617
Partially initialized objects deserve
special attention.

14
00:00:38.697 --> 00:00:41.875
A constructor-style function may allocate
the outer object,

15
00:00:41.955 --> 00:00:46.172
allocate several fields, open related
resources, and then fail halfway through.

16
00:00:46.252 --> 00:00:50.224
The cleanup path must know which fields
are valid and which should be ignored.

17
00:00:50.304 --> 00:00:53.299
Predictable failure handling keeps memory
safety local.

18
00:00:53.379 --> 00:00:57.657
If a function either fully constructs an
object or fully cleans up before returning

19
00:00:57.737 --> 00:00:59.815
failure, callers have a clean contract.

20
00:00:59.895 --> 00:01:02.706
If a function returns an ambiguous
half-owned object,

21
00:01:02.786 --> 00:01:06.208
every caller must understand the partial
state, which is fragile.

22
00:01:06.288 --> 00:01:09.894
Reviewers should look for allocation
checks, initialization order,

23
00:01:09.974 --> 00:01:11.563
cleanup on every failure path,

24
00:01:11.643 --> 00:01:14.699
and ownership transfer only after
construction succeeds.

25
00:01:14.779 --> 00:01:16.918
Failure handling is not an afterthought;

26
00:01:16.998 --> 00:01:20.420
it is one of the main places heap safety
is either preserved or lost.
